        "q": "How do you measure the success of a Transaction Monitoring product, and how would you present those metrics to a bank's Chief Compliance Officer?",
        "tracks": ["Fintech PM", "B2B / Enterprise PM"],
        "subcategory": "metrics",
        "difficulty": "Medium",
        "domain": "fintech",
        "company": "Visa",
        "a": "Before I answer, I want to clarify the audience framing. Is the CCO presentation for a prospective client we are trying to win, an existing client in their quarterly business review, or an internal Visa leadership audience?\n\nI'll assume this is an existing client quarterly business review since that is the highest-stakes version and requires the most carefully chosen metrics.\n\nThe metrics I would present fall into three categories: effectiveness, efficiency, and regulatory defensibility.\n\nFor effectiveness, the headline metric is detection rate, specifically the percentage of confirmed suspicious activity reports that were first identified by the platform rather than by external tips, audit findings, or client complaints. A CCO cares deeply about whether the system is actually finding financial crime, not just generating activity. From data analysis I have done in similar contexts, I noticed that most compliance teams receive this metric as a percentage of SARs filed that originated from system alerts, and anything above 75% is considered strong for a mature programme.\n\nFor efficiency, the metrics I would present are alert volume trend, alert-to-investigation ratio, and average investigation time. These three together tell the story of whether analyst capacity is being used effectively. A falling alert volume with a stable or improving SAR rate is the ideal signature. Rising alert volume without a corresponding SAR increase is the signature of a false positive problem.\n\nFor regulatory defensibility, I would present the platform's audit trail completeness rate, the percentage of closed alerts with documented disposition rationale, and our performance against any outstanding regulatory examination findings.\n\nIn past projects where I have tackled similar challenges, I found that CCOs are most responsive to peer benchmarking. If I can show that our platform's false positive rate is 40% below the industry median for their transaction volume and risk profile, that number lands harder than any absolute metric.\n\nI would structure the presentation as current state, trend over the review period, and one forward-looking commitment for the next quarter. The forward-looking commitment is what CCOs remember from these meetings and what builds long-term trust in the product team."
