        "q": "How would you approach writing a PRD for a new Beneficial Ownership verification feature on Visa's compliance platform?",
        "tracks": ["Fintech PM", "B2B / Enterprise PM"],
        "subcategory": "product_execution",
        "difficulty": "Medium",
        "domain": "fintech",
        "company": "Visa",
        "a": "Before I answer, I want to clarify a couple of scoping questions. Is this PRD for a feature that handles UBO verification for Visa's direct bank clients during their own onboarding, or for a tool that Visa's clients use to verify the beneficial owners of their own customers? And are we building the verification capability in-house or integrating with a third-party UBO data provider?\n\nI'll assume we are building a UBO verification tool for Visa's direct enterprise clients, integrating with a third-party data provider for corporate registry data.\n\nHere is how I would structure the PRD.\n\nThe first section is the problem statement. It needs to be anchored in specific, quantified pain. For this feature, the problem is that enterprise clients are currently spending an average of 4.5 analyst hours per corporate customer onboarding on manual UBO research, creating a 12-business-day onboarding lead time that is causing measurable client loss to faster competitors. That specificity tells engineering exactly what level of automation is needed to make a meaningful difference.\n\nThe second section is user stories, and I would write these from two perspectives: the compliance analyst doing the daily work and the compliance officer who needs to defend the process to a regulator. Those two users have different definitions of done. The analyst needs speed. The compliance officer needs auditability.\n\nFrom user research I have done on similar problems, I found that PRDs that only include the primary user often miss critical requirements around audit trails, exception handling, and reporting that become blocking issues late in development.\n\nThe third section is acceptance criteria, and I am very specific here. I would define: automated UBO identification to the 25% ownership threshold level for standard CDD cases, manual override capability with a documented justification field for complex ownership structures, integration with our existing sanctions and PEP screening pipeline so UBO data is automatically screened during onboarding, and a compliance report export that a client can attach to a regulatory examination response.\n\nIn past projects where I have tackled similar challenges, I found that the most important section of a compliance PRD is the explicitly out-of-scope section. Compliance requirements tend to expand during development because engineers encounter edge cases and ask reasonable questions that were not in the original spec. A clear out-of-scope list stops scope creep before it starts and gives the team a decision framework for handling those questions independently."
